 the matrix $P$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$ is $\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${p}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{p}_{4}}=\\underbrace{(2)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{p}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(2)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{p}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2=4.$"] [17592186484285 "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{5\\times 7}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{5\\times 7}(R))=5\\cdot 7=35.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"] [17592186485526 "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U.\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)U \\\\ C & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486378 "First, notice that  the position vector of the point where $t=-\\pi $ is\n\n$$\np=r(-\\pi )=⟨{sin}^{3}⁡(-\\pi ),{cos}^{3}⁡(-\\pi ),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi )⟩=⟨0,-1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(-\\pi )$ and the principal normal $N(-\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-\\pi )\\times N(-\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,-4⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨0,-1,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}|\\,0-4+3\\,| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486405 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(3) & =T(3)\\times N(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,4⟩\\times \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}⟨(-9-16),-(0-0),(0-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}⟨-25,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486409 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨1,2t,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2t & 0 \\\\ -2t & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2t \\\\ -2t & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0-0,-(0-0),1+4{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0,0,1+4{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486410 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that all linear combinations of \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nresult in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of the set, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186486413 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨(0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),-(0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),(3{sin}^{2}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486416 "First, notice that the position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\np=r(0)=(0+\\sqrt{2})\\,i+(\\sqrt{2}-0)\\,j+0\\,k=⟨\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2},0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(0)\\times N(0) \\\\  & =k\\times (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-\\,j)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2},0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1,0⟩| \\\\  & =|1+1| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486418 "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(1,-1,5)$ is $p=i-j+5\\,k.$\n\nWe need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(0)\\times N(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,-1,3⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\left[\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}(-4\\,i+5\\,j-\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|(i-j+5\\,k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}(-4\\,i+5\\,j-\\,k)| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}|-4-5-5| \\\\  & =\\frac{|-14|}{\\sqrt{42}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{42}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486424 "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(4,0,4)$ is $p=⟨4,\\,0,\\,4⟩.$\n\nWe need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(-2)$ and the principal normal $N(-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(-2)\\times N(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,\\,\\sqrt{2},\\,1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j-\\sqrt{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨4,0,4⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}|4+0-4| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486428 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+5}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+5}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+5}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{10}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{8t}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}},\\,\\frac{4t}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}}⟨5,\\,-4t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{2}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(5)^{2}+(-4t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{25+16{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{20{t}^{2}+25} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{4{t}^{2}+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{2}{(4{t}^{2}+5)^{3/2}}⟨5,\\,-4t,\\,2t⟩}{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{4{t}^{2}+5}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+5}}⟨5,\\,-4t,\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+5}}⟨2t,2,-1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+5}}⟨5,\\,-4t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(4{t}^{2}+5)}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -4t & 2t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(4{t}^{2}+5)}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -4t & 2t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}2t & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(4{t}^{2}+5)}⟨4t-4t,-(4{t}^{2}+5),-8{t}^{2}-10⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(4{t}^{2}+5)}⟨0,\\,-(4{t}^{2}+5),\\,-2(4{t}^{2}+5)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486433 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),2\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & 2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)-0,-(-{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)-{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)),0-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),\\,1,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486436 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨({sin}^{2}⁡t-(-{cos}^{2}⁡t)),-(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-0),(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
